.TH COUNT 1 "2025-01-15" "MPC v0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
count \- count lines, words, or characters in files
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B count
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.RI [ FILE... ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.B count
utility counts lines, words, and characters in the specified files or from
standard input if no files are provided. By default, it displays all three
counts (lines, words, characters) followed by the filename.
.PP
When multiple files are specified,
.B count
displays individual counts for each file, followed by total counts.
.PP
A word is defined as a sequence of characters delimited by whitespace.
A line is terminated by a newline character.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-lines
Count lines only. Display only the number of newline characters.
.TP
.BR \-w ", " \-\-words
Count words only. Display only the number of whitespace-delimited words.
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-chars
Count characters only. Display only the number of characters (including newlines).
.TP
.BR \-b ", " \-\-bytes
Count bytes instead of characters. Useful for binary files or when character
encoding matters.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Display help information and exit.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-version
Display version information and exit.
.SH EXAMPLES
Count all metrics for a single file:
.nf
.RS
$ count file.txt
  10  50 250 file.txt
.RE
.fi
.PP
Count words from standard input:
.nf
.RS
$ echo "hello world" | count --words
2
.RE
.fi
.PP
Count lines in multiple files:
.nf
.RS
$ count --lines *.txt
  10 file1.txt
  20 file2.txt
  30 total
.RE
.fi
.PP
Count characters excluding whitespace:
.nf
.RS
$ count --chars document.txt
1250 document.txt
.RE
.fi
.SH OUTPUT FORMAT
When no specific count option is given, the output format is:
.PP
.I lines words characters filename
.PP
Each count is right-aligned in a field of 8 characters.
When reading from standard input, no filename is displayed.
.PP
With specific count options (
.BR \-l ", " \-w ", " \-c
), only the requested count is displayed, followed by the filename if applicable.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B count
exits with one of the following values:
.TP
.B 0
Success. All files were processed successfully.
.TP
.B 1
File error. One or more files could not be read.
.TP
.B 2
Invalid command line arguments.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.B count
may produce the following error messages:
.TP
.B "count: 'filename': No such file or directory"
The specified file does not exist or cannot be accessed.
.TP
.B "count: 'filename': Permission denied"
The file exists but cannot be read due to insufficient permissions.
.TP
.B "count: invalid option -- 'x'"
An unrecognized command line option was specified.
.SH NOTES
Unlike some implementations,
.B count
treats all whitespace characters (spaces, tabs, newlines) as word delimiters.
.PP
For very large files, counts are performed efficiently using buffered I/O
without loading the entire file into memory.
.PP
Binary files are handled correctly, with byte counts being more meaningful
than character counts for such files.
